From 5664caf9cab1018207312fc8196755a47860658f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:43:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] microblaze: allow 64-bit values in odd register pairs

microblaze_hard_regno_mode_ok, inherited from MIPS, puts DImode and
DFmode values only in even register pairs.  The ABI returns them in
r3:r4 and passes the first in r5:r6, both odd pairs, so every 64-bit
argument and result was copied to or from an even pair with two addk:
long long add (long long a, long long b) { return a + b; } was add and
addc with four copies of the arguments into r4:r5 and r6:r7 and two of
the result to r3:r4.

Let a value of several words start at any general register, as long
as it ends at r31.  A double-word result can then overlap an input by
one word, e.g. r3:r4 computed from r4:r5, so the patterns that write
one word of the result before reading the other word of an input must
check for it.  Audit of the DImode and DFmode patterns:

  adddi3, registers: wrote the low word first.  Now goes through r18,
    like adddi3_carry, when the low word of the result is the high
    word of an input.
  adddi3, constant: already checked (microblaze_dword_overlap_p).
  subdi3, mulsidi3, umulsidi3, usmulsidi3: earlyclobber result.
  adddi3_carry, subdi3_nborrow, *ashldi3_one, *<shiftrt>di3_one:
    already checked.
  negdi2: wrote the low word first.  Now goes through r18.
  *one_cmpldi2: wrote the high word first.  Now writes first the word
    that the other does not read.
  not:DI split: wrote subreg 0 first.  Now writes the second register
    first when the result starts one register above the input, as the
    register move splits do.
  *movdi_internal, *movdf_internal: register moves are split after
    reload, in the safe order; loads already check the address against
    the first register written.  The unsplit movdi register template
    now also orders the words, and has a third operand.
  extendsidi2: reads its input only before writing.
  DImode compares and branches, zero extension and the soft-float
  DFmode operations are expanded a word at a time or are libcalls.

Since microblaze_dword_overlap_p no longer depends on a register rule,
its comment changes too.

a + b, a - b, -a, a << 1 and a + 1 on long long arguments are now two
instructions each, with no copies.  In a QEMU instruction count of a
loop calling three small 64-bit functions 2000 times, the count drops
from 96053 to 76049 on microblaze-elf and to 74049 on microblazeel-elf
at -O2 (98044 to 78042 and 76042 at -Os); 64-bit add, shift, xorshift
and multiply loops, whose values stay in registers, move by a few
instructions.  Compiling gcc.c-torture/execute and gcc.target/microblaze,
1653 files, the text shrinks by 3400 bytes (0.11%) at -O2, 91 files
smaller and 5 larger, and by 4092 bytes at -Os, 92 smaller and 5 larger;
the largest growth, 456 bytes in pr58574.c, is one more __adddf3 call
in each of the 64 cases of a switch, 2 * x no longer being shared.  Built by the compiler, the text of
newlib's libc.a shrinks from 413689 to 413481 bytes, libm.a from 222532
to 220244 and libgcc.a from 77004 to 76812 (microblaze-elf).

others/dword-overlap-run.c forces each one-word overlap, the result
one register below and one above the input, with register variables
for negation, complement, moves, shifts by one, additions of registers
and constants, subtraction and the overflow-checking add and subtract,
and compares the results with the same operations done a word at a
time.  Replacing its r18 sequences with the old ones in the assembly
makes it abort.  others/dword-abi-run.c checks 64-bit and double
arguments and results passed in odd pairs and chains of operations on
them.  isa/dword-odd-pair.c checks that no copies are left.

Tested on microblaze-elf and microblazeel-elf under QEMU:
gcc.c-torture/execute.exp and gcc.target/microblaze, and on
microblaze-elf gcc.target/microblaze with -mno-xl-soft-mul,
-mcpu=v10.0 -mxl-barrel-shift -mno-xl-soft-mul -mxl-pattern-compare
-mxl-multiply-high, -mcpu=v8.50.a -mxl-reorder and -mcpu=v9.6
-mno-xl-soft-mul -mxl-reorder, with no new failures.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

gcc/ChangeLog:

	* config/microblaze/microblaze.cc (microblaze_option_override):
	Let a value of several words start at an odd general register.
	(microblaze_hard_regno_mode_ok): Update the comment.
	(microblaze_dword_overlap_p): Likewise.
	* config/microblaze/microblaze.md (adddi3): Go through r18 when
	the low word of the result is the high word of an input.
	(negdi2): Likewise.
	(*one_cmpldi2): Write first the word that the other does not
	read.
	(not:DI split): Likewise.
	(*movdi_internal): Likewise for register moves, and give them a
	third operand.

gcc/testsuite/ChangeLog:

	* gcc.target/microblaze/isa/dword-odd-pair.c: New test.
	* gcc.target/microblaze/others/dword-abi-run.c: New test.
	* gcc.target/microblaze/others/dword-overlap-run.c: New test.
